er precise but you can consider  on the low end that things like eliza  and ali spot they measure  a relatively low number of features and  they give you a very discreet  output and then you know you can also  run flow cytometry now admittedly  photometry can be  just a handful of antibodies or now you  know with the latest machines  you can push it to quite a few or 20 or  more  and then moving on up you have things  like luminex and mesoscale  and scitops which can measure you know  dozens  literally of parameters and at the very  high end you have things like rna-seq  and mass spec which are entirely  unbiased  but requires substantial amounts of  resources and expertise to analyze this  data  and so you know the nice thing about  cyclops is that it sits sort of  at the nexus of this and it offers  quite a bit of information without  necessarily being onerous  and it on the other side though it does  require some thinking  and some planning more than perhaps you  would normally use on the maliza or  traditional plus cytometry approach and  so one of the major  considerations is when you have the  opportunity to use 40 or more  antibodies in a panel how do you design  that experiment  what sorts of things you're going to  want to measure and of course there's  the concrete hypotheses  and the things that you just have to  have that you know that the reviewers  and that agency is going to ask for  and then depending on how the experiment  has been set up and what sort of  historical information you may have of  primary data that may be available  things like rna seq data or sequencing  data might be informative  whether that's telling you about the hla  types that  are within a population or within a  sample set or telling you about  different cytokines or transcription  factors that may be  changed that you may not necessarily  have included  without that information and then  similarly you know illuminex are alive  today that can also tell you about  changes that