er of mics  across multiple time points  and so on the basis of all of that we  chose to use  scitok and looking at the data the very  first thing that we were able to do was  to just identify major immune  populations and then track them through  the course  of the experiment just asking were there  key changes  anything gross grossly observable that  was  different between the vaccinated and the  naive animals  but this is something that you could  also accomplish with a relatively  modestly sized autonomous panel so  then we wanted to try and extract more  information  out from this cytoplasm experiment so on  the pin on the right there you see the  antibody panel that we used  and i think in pragmatic terms the  number as the number of panel  sorry antibodies increase then the  number of plots by actually a plot that  you're going to have to investigate  grows exponentially right and so it just  wasn't feasible for us  to do manual analysis for every possible  parameter across  every mouse and every time point so we  turn  to the some of the tools that have been  developed and this list is constantly  shifting it seems like every week  there's a new wonderful tool out there  for computational assessment of cyclop  data  but i want to illustrate for you some of  the tools that we use to help  investigate the data that came out from  our experiment  so the first thing i want to show you is  a movie of a  from a clustering algorithm called  vortex and  in this movie you'll see just the cd4  and the cd8 t cells moving to opposite  ends of the screen and basically what's  happening here  is the profile of each individual cell  across all of those different antibody  parameters that you saw  is being used to move each dot  according to the measured features  so that similarly labeled cells  land near each other in the plot and  those that are dissimilar from one  another  land far away from one another and so  what you'll see here  is on the bottom left is where the cd4 t  cells are  and on the bottom and on the top right  is where the cd8 t  cells land within each one of those  clusters though  sits different colored groups  and those represent different subclasses  of cd4 and ctop cells  so how do we use this kind of  information so what we can do is take  that clustering information  and we can look at something called a  parallel coordinate plot where  in the background there in grey you see  the values from  all of the other clusters that were in  the assay  and then that dark line there is the  features of that particular cluster that  i'm illustrating for you there  and then on the right is the frequency  of that cluster  in each individual mouse in that  experiment  and then underneath is the same but for  a different cluster so what you can  immediately see is that custom number  eight was enriched in the vaccinated  animals and  cluster number one was enriched in the  naive animals  and if you want to look more closely you  can see that they're both cd8 t  cells and that cluster 8 is positive for  y6c  cd73 and 62l  while cluster 1 is locking the  y6c and it has instead cd44  so immediately now you can start to get  into fairly granular assessments  so if we make a volcano plot of what the  differentially abundant clusters were  between the two groups we can see that a  number of different clusters come out  and you can take it one step further and  ask  okay on the just on the basis of  the abundance of each one of those  clusters what's the probability that any  individual mouse  as the as the abundance increases will  be assigned to either the vaccinated or  the naive group so basically here you  can see which  which clusters are the most important  for distinguishing the two groups  that's very useful however it makes it  very difficult  to look at the entirety of the  experiment  through all the different time points  and so to address that what we did is we  took  the various different clusters from all  the different time points and we've  divided them into t  cells b-cells and anatomy cells and then  we put them into a heat map and we asked  okay are there classes are there groups  of clusters that my hierarchical  clustering  are very similar to one another although  they're not exactly identical  and i won't walk you through all of  these but the point is we can break  each one of those major cell populations  into different subunits  and then we can take those different  classes and we can ask  any given time point which class of t  cells or which class of b  cells or innate cells were the most  important for determining  group assignment and then on the right  there what you can see is that for  each of the different classes of immune  cells  that were important how do they  distribute across the different groups  through time right so now we're able to  essentially get a sense of the dynamics  of these  important immune populations i think the  other thing to take away from this is  that we can see that  the diversity in the immune response is  already increasing by day 10 and at day  35 you have a fairly  diverse set of immune features that are  distinguishing the two groups  but that by day 44 which is two days  after we challenged  these mice that diversity collapses and  we have a very polarized immune response  that distinguishes the naive animals  from the challenged animals  and then even more so at the conclusion  of the experiment  a few days later  so that's useful to see uh  however you know that's it leaves a lot  of  granular information on the table and so  you're only looking at the changes with  these  large classes of immune cells through  time and it gives you a good sense of  you know major movements but it doesn't  really peel back  things down to the level of granularity  that we wanted so the next thing we did  was we  leveraged the fact that we had all this  phenotypic data how big did the spleens  get how much bacterial burden did they  have h