  constrained as well as  then the limitations of the assays  themselves  once you have all of that data and what  you've decided upon the assays that  you're going to use  another hurdle occurs which is then the  analysis of the data itself  and you know if you've used fairly  straightforward assays  then the app then the analysis of that  data is also fairly straightforward  okay for a few key populations and  cytokines if you're using code cytometry  you correlate that back to different  measured features  then if you have much more complex data  you have to do a qc you have to perhaps  do a batch correction  and then you might want to get into much  more nuanced statistical analyses to  find correlates  that are not just straight ahead group  assignment based assessments  and then finally embedded in all of this  uh is the need to consider things like  the animal efficacy ruling and potential  questions that come from the acquired  agencies for  ultimately for approval and the animal  efficacy rule is  not a straightforward problem to tackle  and especially when generating high  parameter data  the agencies are not necessarily  accustomed to seeing that and so  bearing those constraints in mind is  also important  so when thinking about the different  assays that one might employ when  interrogating their samples out from  aesthetic you could  think about it essentially on a  continuum from ease  versus cost and then hypothesis driven  so you're only measuring a few key  parameters  to an unbiased approach and by ease i  want to be clear that  i don't just mean the ease of conducting  the assay but the entirety of the assay  arc  so how easy is it to generate data to  analyze the data to reproduce it  right and similarly cost doesn't just  consider the cost of running the assay  it's also the time necessary to analyze  those data  the time necessary to set up the assay  and make it robust and reproducible and  so  if we look at this and this is not you  know super precise but you can consider  on the low end that things like eliza  and ali spot they measure  a relatively low number of features and  they give you a very discreet  output and then you know you can also  run flow cytometry now admittedly  photometry can be  just a handful of antibodies or now you  know with the latest machines  you can push it to quite a few or 20 or  more  and then moving on up you have things  like luminex and mesoscale  and scitops which can measure you know  dozens  literally of parameters and at the very  high end you have things like rna-seq  and mass spec which are entirely  unbiased  but requires substantial amounts of  resources and expertise to analyze this  data  and so you know the nice thing about  cyclops is that it sits sort of  at the nexus of this and it offers  quite a bit of information without  necessarily being onerous  and it on the other side though it does  require some thinking  and some planning more than perhaps you  would normally use on the maliza or  traditional plus cytometry approach and  so one of the major  considerations is when you have the  opportunity to use 40 or more  antibodies in a panel how do you design  that experiment  what sorts of things you're going to  want to measure and of course there's  the concrete hypotheses  and the things that you just have to  have that you know that the reviewers  and that agency is going to ask for  and then depending on how the experiment  has been set up and what sort of  historical information you may have of  primary data that may be available  things like rna seq data or sequencing  data might be informative  whether that's telling you about the hla  types that  are within a population or within a  sample set or telling you about  different cytokines or transcription  factors that may be  changed that you may not necessarily  have included  without that information and then  similarly you know illuminex are alive  today that can also tell you about  changes that may not have necessarily  been the first ones that you considered  i think the other thing to point out  here is that in the design of a panel  especially for things like cycle  where possible it's it's always useful  to choose  markers that are multifunctional  somewhat promiscuous  so that it gives you information across  a number of different populations  so once you've designed and validated  your panel and you're able to acquire  the data by  cytometry you know the next challenge  really comes in terms of the analysis  and there for our group we do first a  quick check on the qc of the data  and then we move directly into doing  both manual analysis for those those  hypotheses that we want to see  out early and then we move and at the  same time in parallel we start to  conduct some of the computation analysis  because those can take some time  on the back end from all of that thing  you wind up with things like immune  correlates  that can tell you about which  populations correspond to  particular measures of infection or  particular kinds of immune responses  and then you'll generate from that those  testable observations that you can come  back and test maybe an orthogonal  assay but also you you may will wind up  generating new hypotheses  and so it can become a bit of a loop  here but the point is to identify  what kinds of readouts you need to help  push your platform forward  so i think for us as i mentioned before  relatively speaking in  mass cytometry you can get a high data  density  for the sample volume and i'll talk more  about that in a moment there are  opportunities to mitigate  batch effects and take advantage of the  fact that the cytosk can be very very  consistent from cycle site to site two  things that are much more difficult to  achieve with traditional flow cytometry  another consideration  uh that was important for us was the  fact that fixation is  very much a part of the standard  workflow for scitok and  because uh the example that i mentioned  in a few minutes required that  that really was was very important  for those of you that are new to scitoc  you know it is worth pointing out that  the standard clones that are part of  your favorite flow cytometry panel  most of them if not all of them will  translate over nearly directly  to psychology you have to generate a  whole new class of antibody reagents  and then on top of that cytok does offer  additional  assets things like being able to run  phosphoflow across a number of different  targets whole panels the tetramers might  be available  the ability to analyze whole blood and  so on  so with all of that in mind now i want  to tell you about  a vaccination and challenge today that  we conducted  as part of a new program to  develop a vaccine for a bacterial  infection called  called coccygealo burnetti so cocktail  i don't expect is a bacteria that most  of you familiar with  i think the key things here to  understand that it's a very infectious  bacterial pathogen that's transmitted by  the aerosol route  and it's typically found in ruminants so  in good  shape and what we know from  mouse studies the relatively few that  have been done is that t cells are  required for control and the  b cells are dispensable now there is a  vaccine that is already out there  however that vaccine has its own set of  problems so it's a wholesaling activated  vaccine and in  some individuals it can elicit the  ideogenicity  so these people have adverse events  following vaccination and as a  consequence of that  the vaccine is only approved for use in  a limited basis in australia  however in the last eight to ten years  it's become evident as  military personnel from western nations  have been deployed into endemic areas  that there was a need for a new vaccine  because it's not feasible to  pre-screen an entire battalion of  individuals before they're deployed  and so we were one of several groups  awarded an opportunity to develop  a new vaccine candidate for cocktailer  so i'm not going to spend a lot of time  on this but i do want to point out that  it was part of the big consortium effort  where we needed to determine what  epitopes are  important for the immune response  against coxiella in both humans and  in the mouse model system and ultimately  the objective was to generate  a new epitope-based candidate vaccine  my team one of the principal responses  that we had was to evaluate  the response to the usual vaccine  in a mirroring model so that then we had  a baseline comparator  for the candidate vaccine so the study i  want to tell you about used  transgenic hla dr3 mice so these are  mice with a human mhc allele  on a black sticks background the study  took  four different bleeds from the same ice  it's the longitudinal stages chief leads  about 150 to 200 microliters of blood  per time point  and then we had two groups of mice those  that were vaccinated  and then those that were not and then  those two groups were subdivided  into challenged and not challenged this  was done  in collaboration with our friends at  colorado state  and also out from the study we measured  the usual sorts of things what kind of  antibody tyler was generated how much  splenomegaly occurred what sort of  bacterial burden  did those mice experience and what were  the histopath scores  from various different tissues  from the blood though we had this  challenge of the fact that everything  was being done  in the bsl3 and that at colorado state  they had to prepare the samples for us  and then ship them  here to possible and so  as i mentioned we had these very small  sample volumes we had to get them out  from the dsl3  and validate that those samples were  inactivated and that required a hard  fixation  of over an hour with four percent psa  and we found that that was adversely  impacting the performance of normal  fluorescence-based  antibodies and we also had this  challenge of interrogating  a large relatively large number of mics  across multiple time points  and so on the basis of all of that we  chose to use  scitok and looking at the data the very  first thing that we were able to do was  to just identify major immune  populations and then track them through  the course  of the experiment just asking were there  key changes  anything gross grossly observable that  was  different between the vaccinated and the  naive animals  but this is something that you could  also accomplish with a relatively  modestly sized autonomous panel so  then we wanted to try and extract more  information  out from this cytoplasm experiment so on  the pin on the right there you see the  antibody panel that we used  and i think in pragmatic terms the  number as the number of panel  sorry antibodies increase then the  number of plots by actually a plot that  you're going to have to investigate  grows exponentially right and so it just  wasn't feasible for us  to do manual analysis for every possible  parameter across  every mouse and every time point so we  turn  to the some of the tools that have been  developed and this list is constantly  shifting it seems like every week  there's a new wonderful tool out there  for computational assessment of cyclop  data  but i want to illustrate for you some of  the tools that we use to help  investigate the data that came out from  our experiment  so the first thing i want to show you is  a movie of a  from a clustering algorithm called  vortex and  in this movie you'll see just the cd4  and the cd8 t cells moving to opposite  ends of the screen and basically what's  happening here  is the profile of each individual cell  across all of those different antibody  parameters that you saw  is being used to move each dot  according to the measured features  so that similarly labeled cells  land near each other in the plot and  those that are dissimilar from one  another  land far away from one another and so  what you'll see here  is on the bottom left is where the cd4 t  cells are  and on the bottom and on the top right  is where the cd8 t  cells land within each one of those  clusters though  sits different colored groups  and those represent different subclasses  of cd4 and ctop cells  so how do we use this kind of  information so what we can do is take  that clustering information  and we can look at something called a  parallel coordinate plot where  in the background there in grey you see  the values from  all of the other clusters that were in  the assay  and then that dark line there is the  features of that particular cluster that  i'm illustrating for you there  and then on the right is the frequency  of that cluster  in each individual mouse in that  experiment  and then underneath is the same but for  a different cluster so what you can  immediately see is that custom number  eight was enriched in the vaccinated  animals and  cluster number one was enriched in the  naive animals  and if you want to look more closely you  can see that they're both cd8 t  cells and that cluster 8 is positive for  y6c  cd73 and 62l  while cluster 1 is locking the  y6c and it has instead cd44  so immediately now you can start to get  into fairly granular assessments  so if we make a volcano plot of what the  differentially abundant clusters were  between the two groups we can see that a  number of different clusters come out  and you can take it one step further and  ask  okay on the just on the basis of  the abundance of each one of those  clusters what's the probability that any  individual mouse  as the as the abundance increases will  be assigned to either the vaccinated or  the naive group so basically here you  can see which  which clusters are the most important  for distinguishing the two groups  that's very useful however it makes it  very difficult  to look at the entirety of the  experiment  through all the different time points  and so to address that what we did is we  took  the various different clusters from all  the different time points and we've  divided them into t  cells b-cells and anatomy cells and then  we put them into a heat map and we asked  okay are there classes are there groups  of clusters that my hierarchical  clustering  are very similar to one another although  they're not exactly identical  and i won't walk you through all of  these but the point is we can break  each one of those major cell populations  into different subunits  and then we can take those different  classes and we can ask  any given time point which class of t  cells or which class of b  cells or innate cells were the most  important for determining  group assignment and then on the right  there what you can see is that for  each of the different classes of immune  cells  that were important how do they  distribute across the different groups  through time right so now we're able to  essentially get a sense of the dynamics  of these  important immune populations i think the  other thing to take away from this is  that we can see that  the diversity in the immune response is  already increasing by day 10 and at day  35 you have a fairly  diverse set of immune features that are  distinguishing the two groups  but that by day 44 which is two days  after we challenged  these mice that diversity collapses and  we have a very polarized immune response  that distinguishes the naive animals  from the challenged animals  and then even more so at the conclusion  of the experiment  a few days later  so that's useful to see uh  however you know that's it leaves a lot  of  granular information on the table and so  you're only looking at the changes with  these  large classes of immune cells through  time and it gives you a good sense of  you know major movements but it doesn't  really peel back  things down to the level of granularity  that we wanted so the next thing we did  was we  leveraged the fact that we had all this  phenotypic data how big did the spleens  get how much bacterial burden did they  have how much  antibodies header and for mouse by mouse  by mouse  we measured or we took the information  on the abundance of  every single cluster through the course  of the experiment and we've correlated  that  to each one of these phenotypic measures  and that's what you see on the right is  this  very complex correlation matrix  and so to avoid going cross-side looking  at this information  what we did is we made another  hierarchical distribution  of all of those different clusters and  we were able to break  that correlation matrix into different  modules  on the basis of essentially how similar  was the correlation  between the different cluster clusters  of immune cells  and that was independent of whether it  was a t cell or a b cell and an  immune cell if it correlated to this  phenotypic features in the same way that  it landed in that same module  even still with this it was difficult  for us so we took  [Music]  an approach where we repurposed disney  and rather than using the  phenotypes of the immune cells we use  their correlation features  to make a disney map to reduce the  dimensionality of this and to really  make it much more obvious  which of these modules and which subsets  of  or the contents of each module were  really the most important  in terms of the correlation and so  what i'm showing you here is the  correlation data as it relates to  antibody titer on day 10 and on day 35  and there what you see is essentially  the size and the  color intensity of each one of those  different shapes  it relates back to how strongly  it correlates either positively which is  red or negatively which is blue  to those measured features so you'll  notice that on the bottom left  those cells strongly correlate the  antibody titer on day 10 or on day 35  and not surprisingly those same cells in  the bottom left have an inverse  relationship  relative to how sick the mice got it so  how much effect bacteria did they have  in their swing how big did their sting 